# Template. The real file lives only on the host at /opt/litellm/.env
# (root:root, 0640) and is gitignored -- nothing in this repo deploys this one.
#
# All three are left empty on purpose. compose.yaml reads them as ${VAR:?...},
# and the `:?` form errors on empty as well as unset -- so copying this file
# verbatim stops the stack with a clear message instead of quietly starting the
# gateway on placeholder credentials that are published in this repo.
#
# stacks/litellm/README.md has a one-liner that generates all three at once.

# Admin credential: it gates the /ui dashboard and mints the virtual keys that
# everything else authenticates with. Treat it like a root password. LiteLLM
# requires the `sk-` prefix. Generate with:
#   printf 'sk-%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=

# Encrypts provider API keys at rest in the database. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Set it once and never change it. Rotating this key does not re-encrypt
# anything: every provider key already stored becomes undecryptable and has to
# be pasted in again. It belongs in your password manager next to a note saying
# which database it goes with.
LITELLM_SALT_KEY=

# Password for the bundled Postgres. Never leaves the compose network -- the
# database publishes no port -- but it is a real credential and a rebuild of
# /srv is the only thing that regenerates it. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Hex on purpose: this value is interpolated into DATABASE_URL, so anything
# from the set @ : / # ? % would have to be percent-encoded to survive being
# parsed as a URL. A hex string sidesteps that entirely.
#
# Read at initdb time only. Changing this line after the database exists gives
# you a gateway that cannot log in, not a rotated password; see the README.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Provider keys are deliberately NOT here. STORE_MODEL_IN_DB is on, so models
# and their keys are added through the UI and stored in Postgres encrypted with
# LITELLM_SALT_KEY -- one source of truth, and no secret in a second file.
# To pass one through the environment instead, it has to be added to both this
# file and the `environment:` block in compose.yaml.
